<article class="env-remark" id="0AIE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AIE">Remark <span data-tag="0AIE">87.9.8</span>.</a> The classical affine formal algebraic spaces correspond to the affine formal schemes considered in EGA (<span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/EGA">EGA</a>]</span>). To explain this we assume our base scheme is $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Z})$. Let $\mathfrak X = \text{Spf}(A)$ be an affine formal scheme. Let $h_\mathfrak X$ be its functor of points as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0AI1" data-tag="0AI1">87.2.1</a>. Then $h_\mathfrak X = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits h_{\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A/I)}$ where the colimit is over the collection of ideals of definition of the admissible topological ring $A$. This follows from (<a href="/tag/0AHZ" data-tag="0AHZ">87.2.0.1</a>) when evaluating on affine schemes and it suffices to check on affine schemes as both sides are fppf sheaves, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0AI2" data-tag="0AI2">87.2.2</a>. Thus $h_\mathfrak X$ is an affine formal algebraic space. In fact, it is a classical affine formal algebraic space by Definition <a href="/tag/0AID" data-tag="0AID">87.9.7</a>. Thus Lemma <a href="/tag/0AI1" data-tag="0AI1">87.2.1</a> tells us the category of affine formal schemes is equivalent to the category of classical affine formal algebraic spaces. </p>
</article>